{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nStores that already exist — including client deployments, not just this one — have no\n`.bridge_state/projects.json` and no `bridge_project` on any ticket. Without a migration those stores\nwould read every ticket as having an absent project, and there would be no recorded legacy default\nfor the resolver to fall back to, so a store that syncs perfectly today would stop syncing on upgrade.\n\n## Approach\n\nShip the migration as ensure units on the existing idempotent registry\n(`src/rebar/_store/ensures.py`, `REGISTRY_IDS` at `:66`, `_registry()` at `:82`), which is rebar's\nsanctioned seam: `docs/migrations.md:40-56` describes adding a unit, and the ordered School-A ledger\nis explicitly not built (`docs/migrations.md:14-16`). Units run at init/re-init, `fsck --repair`, and\nMCP boot (`docs/migrations.md:57-65`), so a client store converges without any operator action.\n\n**The migration writes no ticket events.** A unit emitting an EDIT per ticket would be a four-figure\nevent write under the store write lock on a store this size, and the MCP-boot entry point runs the\nsweep on a deliberately short lock budget and skips when contended (`docs/migrations.md:63-65`) — so\nan event-writing migration would silently skip at one of its three entry points and converge\nunpredictably. Instead the unit records `legacy_default` once in the mapping, and the tri-state\nresolver treats every ticket with an absent field as belonging to it. One small write migrates the\nwhole store.\n\nTwo units, both check-then-act and both safe to re-run:\n\n1. **Seed the mapping.** If `.bridge_state/projects.json` is absent, write it with `legacy_default`\n   and a single `projects` entry taken from the backend's configured project\n   (`Backend.project`, the effective write/create scope at `_backend.py:598-605`). If the record\n   already exists, return `ok` and write nothing. Tree-check the committed\n   `tickets:.bridge_state/projects.json` blob before committing so a converged store makes zero git\n   commits, as `docs/migrations.md:45-46` requires of commit units.\n2. **Stamp the capability.** When — and only when — the mapping holds more than one project, add\n   `multi-project-bridge` to `.store-compat.json`'s `required_capabilities`. A single-project store is\n   never stamped and stays usable by older binaries. The `multi-project-bridge` token is **already\n   registered** in `KNOWN_CAPABILITIES` (`src/rebar/_store/compat.py:63`) by the closed dependency\n   67f9, so a store that stamps it still passes its own `check_store_compat` gate on this binary —\n   this story therefore makes **no** edit to `compat.py`. A test asserts a two-project stamped store\n   passes the gate, guarding against a future rename that would fail the store closed against the very\n   binary that stamped it.\n\nIds are new and never reused, per `docs/migrations.md:50-51`.\n\n**Where the unit bodies live.** The existing six units are implemented in `src/rebar/_commands/init.py`\n(for example `_store_compat_unit` at `:509`) and referenced from `_registry()`\n(`src/rebar/_store/ensures.py:82`). This story places the two new unit bodies in their own\n`src/rebar/_store/project_ensures.py` rather than in `init.py`. `init.py` measures **758 lines** — it\nhas ~42 lines of headroom under the hard 800-line module-size gate\n(`.github/module-size-limit.txt`), so a separate module is a **cohesion** choice, not a size-forced\none: the projects-mapping migration is a distinct concern from init's `.gitignore`/`.gitattributes`\ncontent constants, a dedicated module keeps `init.py` from drifting toward the cap, and it groups the\ntwo related units together. Because `_registry()` maps an id to any callable, `ensures.py`\n(257 lines) gains only its two registry entries.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_store/project_ensures.py` (new) — both unit bodies\n- `src/rebar/_store/ensures.py` — two `REGISTRY_IDS` entries and two `_registry()` mappings\n- `docs/migrations.md` — document the two new units and the meaning of the `multi-project-bridge`\n  capability token for other binaries/operators (it gates `.store-compat.json` compatibility)\n- tests under `tests/`\n\n## Testing\n\nTier per the shared standard's git row — real temporary git repositories and refs — because the\nunits commit to the `tickets` branch and the zero-commit-when-converged property is only observable\nagainst real refs.\n\n- Happy path: sweeping a store with no mapping creates one whose `legacy_default` equals the\n  configured backend project, and every existing ticket resolves to it.\n- The load-bearing assertion: the sweep writes **zero ticket events**. Assert the store's event count\n  and the set of event files are identical before and after — this is what distinguishes this design\n  from an EDIT-per-ticket migration.\n- Idempotence: a second sweep on a converged store returns `ok` for both units and makes zero new git\n  commits (assert the branch tip SHA is unchanged, not merely that no error was raised).\n- Conditional stamp, as a contrast pair: a one-project mapping leaves `required_capabilities` empty; a\n  two-project mapping adds exactly `multi-project-bridge`. The one-project half proves the stamp is\n  genuinely conditional rather than unconditional.\n- Compat-gate safety: a two-project store that has stamped `multi-project-bridge` still passes\n  `check_store_compat` on this binary — proves the stamp does not fail the store closed against the\n  binary that wrote it.\n- Failure path: a unit raising is recorded as `failed` and does not abort the sweep or the caller, per\n  the registry's skip-and-continue contract.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A store with no mapping gains one whose `legacy_default` equals the configured backend project;\n      checked by a real-git test reading the seeded record.\n- [ ] Every pre-existing ticket (absent `bridge_project`) resolves to that legacy default; checked by\n      the same test via `resolve_project`.\n- [ ] The sweep writes zero ticket events; checked by asserting the event file set is identical before\n      and after the sweep.\n- [ ] A second sweep returns `ok` for both units; checked by the returned outcome statuses.\n- [ ] A second sweep leaves the tickets-branch tip SHA unchanged; checked by comparing the SHA across\n      two sweeps.\n- [ ] A one-project mapping leaves `required_capabilities` empty; checked by a real-git test.\n- [ ] A two-project mapping adds exactly `multi-project-bridge` to `required_capabilities`; checked by\n      a real-git test.\n- [ ] A two-project store that has stamped `multi-project-bridge` still passes `check_store_compat` on\n      this binary; checked by a test invoking the compat gate.\n- [ ] Both unit ids are registered in `REGISTRY_IDS` and mapped in `_registry()`; checked by the\n      existing registry-agreement test (`test_registry_id_set_is_frozen`), which fails if they disagree.\n- [ ] A unit that raises is recorded `failed` and does not abort the sweep or its caller; checked by\n      monkeypatching a raising unit into `_registry()`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786664504794737001,"uuid":"c8b83c65-25a6-4cf7-b712-6b367426dada"}